<header class="ref-head">
  <p class="eyebrow">Libraries</p>
  <h1>Libraries, step by step</h1>
  <p class="lede">Build services, coordinate work and manage application data. Follow practical guides for web APIs, AI, databases and streams, then add feature flags, privacy controls, deployment and recovery.</p>
  <p class="ref-note">Example files live in <a href="https://github.com/marcrabadan/nexo/tree/main/docs/libraries"><code>docs/libraries</code></a> in the repository checkout. Unless a command says otherwise, run it from its example’s folder. Displayed results come from executable examples; validation uses isolated copies so databases, exports and local state stay separate. No external provider is called by the executed examples. C/Python examples require their host integration tooling.</p>
  <p class="ref-note">Adding credentials or model tools? Follow the <a href="security.html">security guide</a> to check permissions, verify a denial and constrain an agent’s tools before connecting external services. The <a href="reference.html#official">reference</a> lists module options and language constructs.</p>
</header>

<section class="ref-sec" id="web">
  <h2><a href="#web"><code>nexo.web</code> and HTTP: build and test an API</a></h2>
  <p>A <code>server</code> block defines routes; <code>http</code> calls them as a client. Start with a small registration endpoint, verify its inputs and responses, then add authentication. These examples return the submitted record; they do not create an account, persist data or send an email.</p>

  <h3 id="web-setup">Run locally, test on a free port</h3>
  <p>No <code>use</code> or external service is needed for these APIs. From the repository checkout, run the first example’s tests:</p>
  <div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>From the repository root</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code>nexo test docs/libraries/web/routes.nexo</code></pre></div>
  <p>In a test, Nexo starts the server on a free local port; <code>signup.url</code> contains its address. Use that URL rather than assuming port 8080. To try the example outside tests, run <code>nexo docs/libraries/web/routes.nexo</code>, visit <code>http://localhost:8080/health</code>, then stop it with Ctrl-C. The routes in this first example have no authentication; use them for the local exercise.</p>

  <h3 id="web-routes">Typed routes and request bodies</h3>
  <p><code>get "/greet/{id}"(id: int, name: str = "visitor")</code> takes <code>id</code> from the path and <code>name</code> from the query string. A <code>post</code> route with a record parameter validates the JSON body against that record before entering the handler. A missing required field or invalid typed path returns 400. A <code>str</code> field checks its type; it does not establish that an email address exists or belongs to the caller.</p>
  <p>A text result becomes a text response. A record becomes JSON, with status 201 for a successful POST. The client’s <code>.status</code>, <code>.text</code> and <code>.json()</code> let tests inspect the response. These tests make real loopback requests, including invalid ones:</p>
{{test:web/routes.nexo}}

  <h3 id="web-auth">Authentication and roles</h3>
  <p>Set <code>auth = tokens</code> to require a JWT by default. Mark only intentionally open routes with <code>@public</code>; use <code>@role("admin")</code> when a valid token also needs an admin role. The tests below distinguish a missing token (401) from a valid token with the wrong role (403), then verify the allowed request.</p>
  <p>The example creates a fresh signing key and signs tokens directly inside tests. It is not a login implementation. For deployment, use a managed, stable signing secret through <code>jwt(key: secret("JWT_KEY"))</code>, and issue a role only after your application has authenticated the user and established that role. Do not accept a caller-supplied role as authority. Follow the <a href="security.html#next">secrets and security references</a> before connecting real users.</p>
{{test:web/auth.nexo}}

  <h3 id="web-client">HTTP failures, timeouts and retries</h3>
  <p>An HTTP response with status 400 or higher raises <code>HTTPError</code>; connection failures raise <code>NetworkError</code>. Use <code>try</code>/<code>else</code> when you expect an error response. In the examples, <code>fail</code> after each request ensures that a missing rejection fails the test instead of silently passing.</p>
  <p>Requests accept <code>headers:</code>, <code>timeout:</code> and <code>retries:</code>. The default timeout is 30 seconds. GET, PUT and DELETE retry twice after a network error or a 502, 503 or 504. POST and PATCH do not retry by default because repeating them may repeat an effect. Set an appropriate timeout for the dependency you are calling; decide how duplicate writes will be handled before adding retries.</p>
  <p><code>fake</code> can replace a dependency in a test without contacting its service. Keep real HTTP tests for route validation and authentication, and use fakes when testing application logic around an unrelated external dependency. See the <a href="reference.html#std-http">HTTP client and fake example</a> for the call syntax.</p>

  <h3 id="web-next">Add persistence and execution boundaries</h3>
  <p>Continue with <a href="#db-web">a web API backed by a database table</a> when registrations must survive a restart. For all route options and error mappings, see the <a href="reference.html#mod-web">server reference</a>.</p>
  <p>JWT authentication controls who can call a route. Shield controls the protected operations the program can perform. Follow the <a href="security.html">security guide</a> for grants, known effects and denials. The ordinary HTTP examples here are not Shield deployment profiles: dependency/authorization configurations reject external listeners; supported in-memory and loopback ingress profiles require <a href="https://github.com/marcrabadan/nexo/blob/main/docs/shield-web.md">explicit configuration</a>. The offline Linux worker denies sockets.</p>
</section>

<section class="ref-sec" id="ai">
  <h2><a href="#ai"><code>nexo.ai</code>: language models</a></h2>
  <p><code>ask</code> is a function: it sends an instruction and an input to a model and returns its answer. Assigned to a typed variable, the answer is that type, checked field by field. It needs no <code>use</code>.</p>

  <h3 id="ai-setup">Set up a model</h3>
  <p><code>nexo ai setup</code> asks for a provider, a model and the name of the environment variable that holds the key, and saves them for every program of your user. The key itself is never written in a file.</p>
<div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>terminal</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code>$ nexo ai setup
Provider (anthropic, openai, ollama, mistral, gemini, azure or bedrock): anthropic
Model (for example claude-opus): claude-opus
Environment variable that holds the key (for example ANTHROPIC_KEY): ANTHROPIC_KEY
Address (Enter for the usual one):
Configuration saved in ~/.config/nexo/ai.toml
$ export ANTHROPIC_KEY=...</code></pre></div>
  <p>To run models on your own machine, without a key or an account, install <a href="https://ollama.com">Ollama</a>, run <code>ollama pull llama3.2</code> and answer <code>ollama</code> and <code>llama3.2</code>. Any server that speaks OpenAI's API (Groq, OpenRouter, vLLM, LM Studio) is <code>openai</code> with its address.</p>
  <p>A project can have its own settings in a <code>nexo.toml</code>, which applies to every program in its folder and below. <code>[ai.prices]</code> gives the price of each model, so that <code>budget</code> can count the spend; copy them from your provider's price list. <code>[mcp.NAME]</code> declares an MCP server whose tools an agent can use.</p>
{{src:setup/nexo.toml}}
  <p>A program can set the model itself with an <code>ai:</code> block. The settings come from the first place that has them: the call, its <code>profile:</code>, the program's <code>ai</code> block, <code>nexo.toml</code>, then the user's configuration. A <code>policy:</code> block says which hosts and variables the program may use; a release build needs one.</p>
{{test:setup/assistant.nexo}}
{{steps:policy show setup/assistant.nexo}}
  <p>A test with <code>fake ask returns ...</code> never calls the model, so tests run offline, without a key and at no cost. Outside a test, the same program calls the model and <code>nexo ai usage</code> shows the calls, tokens and spend of each day.</p>

  <h3 id="ai-typed">Typed answers</h3>
  <p>The type of the variable tells the model what to answer, and the answer is checked against it: <code>team</code> is one of three words and <code>urgency</code> a number from 1 to 5. An answer that does not fit is sent back to the model with the reason, up to 3 times, and then the call fails with <code>InvalidData</code>. The rest of the program works with an ordinary record.</p>
{{test:ai/triage.nexo}}

  <h3 id="ai-extract">Extract data from text</h3>
  <p>A typed answer can hold dates, money and lists of records, so text that people write becomes data a program can add up. The input goes to the model marked as data, never as instructions, which is the default guard against prompt injection, and <code>@private</code> fields are hidden from it.</p>
{{test:ai/extract.nexo}}

  <h3 id="ai-stream">Answers as they arrive</h3>
  <p>With <code>stream: true</code> the answer comes in pieces, and a <code>for</code> loop runs for each one as it arrives, so a person can start reading at once. A fake answer that is a list of texts gives those pieces.</p>
{{test:ai/stream.nexo}}

  <h3 id="ai-agent">Agents and tools</h3>
  <p>After learning the tool syntax below, use the <a href="security.html#tools">operator-controlled tool example</a> to add narrow grants and human approval. Faking an answer tests application logic; real dispatch permissions must be checked separately.</p>
  <p>A function marked <code>@tool "what it does"</code> can be called by the model, with arguments checked against its parameter types. An <code>agent</code> block gives a model a goal, its tools and a limit of steps; a tool in <code>confirm</code> runs only after a person answers yes on the terminal. The tools are ordinary functions that you test directly, and <code>fake support.handle</code> replaces the agent's answer.</p>
{{test:ai/agent.nexo}}
</section>

<section class="ref-sec" id="db">
  <h2><a href="#db"><code>nexo.db</code>: databases</a></h2>
  <p><code>@table</code> types are the schema, and a pipeline on a table becomes one parameterised SQL query. The same program runs on SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL, MongoDB and Elasticsearch: only the address changes.</p>

  <h3 id="db-setup">Connect a database</h3>
  <p>SQLite is built in: <code>connect("shop.db")</code> opens the file, or creates it, and <code>connect(":memory:")</code> keeps the database in memory. For a server, the address names the user, host and database, and the password goes in <code>key:</code>, from an environment variable. A password written in the address is an error.</p>
<div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>the address of each database</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code class="nexo">use nexo.db

db = connect(&quot;shop.db&quot;)                                                         # SQLite
db = connect(&quot;postgres://shop@db.example.com/shop&quot;, key: secret(&quot;DB_PASS&quot;))      # PostgreSQL 14+
db = connect(&quot;mysql://shop@db.example.com/shop&quot;, key: secret(&quot;DB_PASS&quot;))         # MySQL 8+
db = connect(&quot;mongodb://shop@db1.example.com,db2.example.com/shop&quot;, key: secret(&quot;DB_PASS&quot;))
db = connect(&quot;elasticsearch://elastic@search.example.com:9200/shop&quot;, key: secret(&quot;ES_PASS&quot;))</code></pre></div>
  <p>Nexo speaks each database's own protocol, so there is no driver to install. A server on another machine is reached over TLS, with its certificate checked against the system's authorities; <code>SSL_CERT_FILE</code> adds a certificate of your own. On your own machine, a server without TLS works too:</p>
<div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>terminal</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code>$ docker run -d -p 5432:5432 -e POSTGRES_USER=shop -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=dev postgres:16
$ DB_PASS=dev nexo app.nexo     # with connect(&quot;postgres://shop@localhost/shop&quot;, key: secret(&quot;DB_PASS&quot;))</code></pre></div>
  <p>The policy of a program that uses a server names its host and the variable of the password: <code>allow net "db.example.com"</code> and <code>allow env "DB_PASS"</code>. On MongoDB and Elasticsearch, <code>db.sql</code> is an error, there is no schema to migrate, and a transaction undoes its writes when it fails but does not hide them from other programs before it ends.</p>

  <h3 id="db-queries">Tables and queries</h3>
  <p><code>ref</code> is a link to a row of another table and <code>many</code> the rows that link back. <code>where</code>, <code>sort by</code>, <code>take</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>pick</code> and <code>group by</code> run in the database, also through a <code>ref</code> (<code>where member.city == "Rome"</code>); a step that cannot be translated runs in memory, with the same result.</p>
{{run:db/library.nexo}}

  <h3 id="db-transactions">Transactions</h3>
  <p>Everything inside <code>db.transaction:</code> is kept or undone together. The third transfer has already taken the money from Ana when it finds that Eva has no account, and the failure undoes that too. <code>expect</code> checks the amount before anything starts.</p>
{{run:db/bank.nexo}}

  <h3 id="db-sql">The SQL it sends</h3>
  <p>Totals and groups are computed by the database, not by reading every row. <code>NEXO_SQL_LOG=1</code> writes each statement to the error output, with its parameters: values never become part of the SQL text.</p>
{{run:db/report.nexo}}
{{sql:db/report.nexo}}

  <h3 id="db-web">A web API on a table</h3>
  <p>A <code>server</code> block and a table make a small API. A body is checked against its type, a missing row (<code>find(...)?</code>) is a 404, and <code>nexo test</code> starts the server on a free port.</p>
{{test:db/notes.nexo}}

  <h3 id="db-migrate">Migrations</h3>
  <p>A table is created the first time a program uses it, and a new field adds its column. A change that could lose data waits for a migration: <code>nexo db diff</code> compares the types with the database and writes the SQL to a file you can read, and <code>nexo db migrate</code> applies it in a transaction. Removing a column needs <code>--allow-drop</code>.</p>
{{src:migrate/shop.nexo}}
{{src:migrate/shop_v2.nexo}}
{{steps:migrate/shop.nexo; db diff migrate/shop_v2.nexo; db migrate migrate/shop_v2.nexo; migrate/shop_v2.nexo; db diff migrate/shop_v2.nexo}}
</section>

<section class="ref-sec" id="stream">
  <h2><a href="#stream"><code>nexo.stream</code>: real-time data</a></h2>
  <p>A stream is one more source for <code>when</code> and <code>|&gt;</code>. <code>when</code> handles each message; a pipeline groups them by time. A message whose block fails is tried 4 times and then goes to the dead letters, <code>NAME.dead</code>, so one bad message never stops the others.</p>

  <h3 id="stream-setup">Connect a broker</h3>
  <p>A source without a broker address is on the local broker, which needs no setup: files in <code>.nexo/streams</code> shared by the programs of your machine. That is enough to develop, and <code>nexo test</code> uses an in-memory broker that starts empty for each test. An option of a broker or a cloud puts the same source there, and nothing else in the program changes:</p>
<div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>the sources of each broker</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code class="nexo">use nexo.stream

kafka(&quot;orders&quot;)                                              # the local broker
kafka(&quot;orders&quot;, partitions: 4)                               # local, 4 partitions
kafka(&quot;orders&quot;, servers: &quot;shop@kafka1.example.com:9092,kafka2.example.com:9092&quot;, key: secret(&quot;KAFKA_PASS&quot;))
rabbit(&quot;orders&quot;, url: &quot;amqps://shop@mq.example.com/&quot;, key: secret(&quot;RABBIT_PASS&quot;))
eventhub(&quot;clicks&quot;, namespace: &quot;shop&quot;)                        # Azure Event Hubs
servicebus(&quot;orders&quot;, namespace: &quot;shop&quot;)                      # Azure Service Bus
sqs(&quot;orders&quot;, region: &quot;eu-west-1&quot;)                           # AWS SQS
kinesis(&quot;clicks&quot;, region: &quot;eu-west-1&quot;)                       # AWS Kinesis
pubsub(&quot;orders&quot;, project: &quot;shop&quot;)                            # Google Cloud Pub/Sub
websocket(&quot;wss://feed.example.com/prices&quot;)</code></pre></div>
  <p><code>key:</code> is the broker's password, from an environment variable. A broker on another machine is reached over TLS only. The clouds use the credentials of <code>nexo.infra</code>: <code>AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID</code> and <code>AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY</code>; <code>AZURE_TENANT_ID</code>, <code>AZURE_CLIENT_ID</code> and <code>AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET</code>; <code>GOOGLE_OAUTH_ACCESS_TOKEN</code>. The queues and topics must exist: an <code>infra</code> block's <code>queue</code> creates them. To try Kafka or RabbitMQ on your own machine:</p>
<div class="code"><div class="code-bar"><span>terminal</span><button class="copy" type="button">Copy</button></div><pre><code>$ docker run -d -p 9092:9092 apache/kafka:3.8.0     # kafka(&quot;orders&quot;, servers: &quot;localhost:9092&quot;)
$ docker run -d -p 5672:5672 rabbitmq:3             # rabbit(&quot;orders&quot;, url: &quot;amqp://guest@localhost/&quot;, key: secret(&quot;RABBIT_PASS&quot;))</code></pre></div>
  <p>The policy names the broker's host: <code>allow net "kafka1.example.com"</code>.</p>

  <h3 id="stream-once">Each message once</h3>
  <p>A broker delivers a message at least once, so after a failure it can arrive twice. With <code>key:</code>, a key already handled is acknowledged without running the block again. <code>validate</code> turns the JSON of a message into a record, and a message that does not fit goes to the dead letters with the reason.</p>
{{test:stream/payments.nexo}}

  <h3 id="stream-windows">Windows of time</h3>
  <p>A pipeline on a stream groups its messages by the time they happened: <code>window 1m</code> by minute, <code>sliding 5m</code> over the last five minutes on every message, and <code>session 30s</code> until 30 seconds pass without a message. In a test, <code>send(..., at: time)</code> sets the time of each message.</p>
{{test:stream/windows.nexo}}

  <h3 id="stream-partitions">Partitions into a table</h3>
  <p>With several partitions, each one is handled by its own task, in parallel, and the messages of one partition stay in order. The <code>key:</code> of <code>send</code> chooses the partition, so the readings of one sensor are handled in order. Here each reading is saved with <code>nexo.db</code>.</p>
{{test:stream/sensors.nexo}}

  <h3 id="stream-processes">Producer and consumer</h3>
  <p>A program with <code>when</code> handlers keeps reading until it is stopped. It saves the position it reached, so a second run reads only new messages. One program sends and another, in another terminal, reads:</p>
{{src:stream/producer.nexo}}
{{src:stream/consumer.nexo}}
{{steps:stream/producer.nexo; ~3 stream/consumer.nexo; ~3 stream/consumer.nexo}}
  <p>The second run of the consumer prints nothing new: the three orders were acknowledged. The same two programs work on Kafka, RabbitMQ or a cloud queue by adding the broker's option to <code>kafka("orders")</code>.</p>
</section>

<section class="ref-sec" id="sync">
  <h2><a href="#sync"><code>nexo.sync</code>: coordinate concurrent work</a></h2>
  <p>Use workers when independent tasks need to exchange results. Channels carry messages, <code>select</code> waits for a message or timeout, and <code>shared</code> marks state that tasks may change together. Start with a fixed number of workers and an explicit way to know when their work is complete.</p>

  <h3 id="sync-workers">A bounded worker queue</h3>
  <p><code>use nexo.sync</code> enables these constructs. <code>spawn f(args)</code> launches a task and lets its caller continue. It does not return a join handle; the process waits for spawned tasks before normal shutdown. In this example, receiving one subtotal from each worker gives the main task an explicit completion point.</p>
  <p><code>channel[int](size: 2)</code> holds at most two queued integers. <code>send</code> waits when the queue is full, which prevents this producer from running arbitrarily far ahead. <code>receive</code> waits when the queue is empty. Two workers consume each job once between them; a channel is not a broadcast.</p>
{{run:sync/workers.nexo}}
  <p>The producer closes the jobs channel after its final send. Workers drain the remaining values and then leave their <code>for</code> loops. Each sends exactly one subtotal. The division of jobs between workers may vary, but the combined total is always 42. Only the main task prints, so scheduling does not change the displayed result.</p>
  <p>Closure means “no more messages”; it does not discard queued values. Sending after closure is an error. Give one producer responsibility for closing the queue, or coordinate multiple producers before closing it. An empty queue with no future sender and no closure can leave a consumer waiting.</p>

  <h3 id="sync-select">Wait for a message or a timeout</h3>
  <p><code>select</code> can wait on several channel branches. A branch receives one available message. An <code>after</code> branch provides a timeout when no message arrives. Here the first selection consumes the queued alert; the second reaches its timeout because the queue is now empty.</p>
{{run:sync/select.nexo}}
  <p>A timeout ends this wait; it does not cancel a worker or close its channel. Arrange shutdown separately. Do not depend on a particular task scheduling order when several sources can produce messages.</p>

  <h3 id="sync-shared">Share state deliberately</h3>
  <p>Ordinary task-owned values are not a place for workers to accumulate shared mutations. Prefer channels for passing results. When tasks need a common map, declare it with <code>shared</code>. Statements that use shared state run under the shared lock; that does not turn several separate statements into a transaction.</p>
{{run:sync/shared.nexo}}
  <p>The <code>parallel for</code> finishes before the assertions run. Each iteration writes a distinct key, so the final map has six entries regardless of scheduling. Keep shared operations short and avoid relying on map iteration order. See the <a href="reference.html#concurrency">concurrency reference</a> for the surrounding language constructs.</p>

  <h3 id="sync-deadlocks">Recognize a queue deadlock</h3>
  <p>A bounded queue needs a receiver that can make progress. In this deliberately failing example, the first send fills the only slot. The second send waits for room, but there is no other task to receive anything.</p>
{{run:sync/deadlock.nexo}}
  <p>This command is expected to exit with code 1. Nexo reports that every task is waiting instead of hanging indefinitely on these channel waits. Start a receiver before filling the queue, or structure the producer/consumer lifecycle as in the worker example. Increasing the buffer only postpones this design error. Deadlock detection is not a general timeout for arbitrary blocking external operations.</p>

  <h3 id="sync-boundaries">Choose sync, streams or an execution boundary</h3>
  <p>Channels coordinate tasks inside one running process. They do not provide a durable broker, replay after a restart or cross-process delivery. Use <a href="#stream">streams and message brokers</a> for those requirements. There is no task handle or per-task cancellation API in this interface.</p>
  <p>Under Shield, spawned tasks retain the caller’s captured permission ceilings; launching a task cannot regain broader root permissions. Configured dependency/agent/tool boundaries restrict messages and shared values to supported plain data. The Linux worker profiles reject threads and sync imports. See the <a href="https://github.com/marcrabadan/nexo/blob/main/docs/shield-spawn.md">spawn security contract</a> and <a href="security.html">security guide</a> before choosing a deployment profile.</p>
</section>

<section class="ref-sec" id="flags">
  <h2><a href="#flags">Feature flags: roll out a change deliberately</a></h2>
  <p>A flag changes which application path runs without changing its source. Flags are available implicitly: declare a <code>flags:</code> block, then call <code>flag(name, user?)</code>. Use a flag for rollout decisions; keep authorization in your authentication and permission checks.</p>
  <h3 id="flags-cohorts">Switches, audiences and stable cohorts</h3>
  <p><code>on</code> and <code>off</code> are global switches. <code>users where</code> evaluates a condition against the supplied user. A percentage chooses a stable cohort using the flag name and user identity, rather than making a fresh random decision for each request. Supply a stable user ID; 10% does not mean exactly one in every ten requests or exactly 10% of a small sample.</p>
{{run:flags/rollout.nexo}}
  <h3 id="flags-overrides">Change the local override</h3>
  <p>Run these commands in the example folder. The first invocation uses the declared default. The setter writes an override to the local <code>.nexo/flags.json</code> store; the next invocation reads it. This example does not contact a hosted flag service.</p>
{{steps:flags/rollout.nexo; flags set checkout on flags/rollout.nexo; flags/rollout.nexo}}
  <p>Use <code>nexo flags set checkout off rollout.nexo</code> to switch it back. Add <code>--stage prod</code> to set a production-stage override; the running application must select the matching stage, such as through <code>NEXO_STAGE=prod</code>. Keep stage and working-directory configuration consistent between the setter and application. Protect the override store from untrusted writes.</p>
  <h3 id="flags-lifecycle">Retire flags after the rollout</h3>
  <p><code>expires:</code> lets lint report a flag past its intended lifetime; it is not an automatic kill switch. Test both application paths, decide what happens when the feature is disabled, and remove the flag when its migration is complete. See the <a href="reference.html#mod-flags">flag syntax reference</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="ref-sec" id="privacy">
  <h2><a href="#privacy">Privacy: know where personal data goes</a></h2>
  <p>Mark data at its record definition, then follow it through storage, logs, export and deletion. These operations help implement application behavior; they do not establish a complete privacy policy or manage copies held by other systems.</p>
  <h3 id="privacy-records">Retention, export, erasure and restriction</h3>
  <p><code>@personal</code> identifies personal fields. <code>purpose</code> records why a type is retained. <code>keep = 2 years after last_seen</code> makes old rows eligible for removal by the runtime’s retention sweep when the table is used, with subsequent sweeps at its configured interval. It is not a separate background deletion service.</p>
  <p>The example uses future dates for its two fixtures so they remain outside the retention cutoff. It declares a retention rule but does not force a sweep or claim that an old row inserted after a sweep disappears immediately. It creates only a temporary local SQLite database when run by this documentation’s validation. Running the source yourself creates files in your working directory.</p>
{{run:privacy/records.nexo}}
  <p><code>privacy.export</code> writes a JSON export containing personal data: handle that file as sensitive. Erasure removes or anonymizes the record according to the supported storage semantics. Restriction hides the remaining record from ordinary queries, but does not physically delete it: the raw SQL count deliberately shows that one row remains. Direct SQL and external consumers need their own controls.</p>
  <h3 id="privacy-logs">Private fields and logs</h3>
  <p><code>@private</code> adds redaction when a record is passed to <code>log</code>. This example uses a dummy token:</p>
{{run:privacy/logging.nexo}}
  <p>Redaction is not general information-flow tracking: do not extract a private field and print or concatenate it into a message. For database storage, <code>@private</code> columns require the connection’s encryption key, for example <code>connect("contacts.db", key: secret("DB_KEY"))</code>. Keep that key outside source and manage its availability separately. The <a href="reference.html#std-crypto">secret and cryptography APIs</a> explain supported consumers.</p>
  <h3 id="privacy-report">Inspect the declared data model</h3>
{{steps:privacy report privacy/records.nexo}}
  <p>The report describes declared fields, retention and purpose. It is not a discovery scan of every file, log, backup or downstream service. See the <a href="reference.html#mod-flags">privacy reference</a> and <a href="security.html">security guide</a> for related controls.</p>
</section>

<section class="ref-sec" id="deployment">
  <h2><a href="#deployment"><code>nexo.infra</code> and <code>nexo.ci</code>: inspect, verify, then deploy</a></h2>
  <p>Infrastructure declarations describe resources and stage overrides. A pipeline sequences work. Keep source validation, local pipeline execution, export and real infrastructure changes separate so the command you run matches the operation you intend.</p>
  <h3 id="deployment-infra">Declare an application and its production stage</h3>
{{src:deployment/deploy.nexo}}
  <p>This Kubernetes example names an image and namespace; it does not build that image. The production stage changes the replica count. Check the source without contacting a cluster:</p>
{{steps:check deployment/deploy.nexo}}
  <p><code>nexo plan deploy.nexo --stage prod</code> is a provider operation: it queries the configured cluster to compare desired and actual resources. It needs the relevant provider credentials and network access. <code>apply</code> and <code>destroy</code> can modify real resources; they are not part of this documentation’s executed examples. Review the plan, image availability, namespace, secrets and target environment before choosing those commands. See the <a href="reference.html#mod-infra">provider and deployment reference</a> for supported resources and configuration.</p>
  <h3 id="deployment-ci">Run a local verification pipeline</h3>
  <p>This pipeline uses a fixed version, checks a function and passes an artifact name between steps. It does not run a shell command, build an image or publish anything.</p>
{{src:deployment/pipeline.nexo}}
{{steps:ci run verify deployment/pipeline.nexo}}
  <p>For an actual build, add the supported <code>test()</code> or <code>build_image(...)</code> operation with its required tools and permissions. Steps that execute commands need the corresponding policy, including <code>allow shell</code> where required. Use <code>approve(by: [...])</code> before a sensitive step; do not treat <code>--yes</code> as evidence that a human reviewed a change.</p>
  <h3 id="deployment-export">Generate a CI configuration without publishing it</h3>
{{steps:ci export github verify deployment/pipeline.nexo}}
  <p>This writes <code>.github/workflows/verify.yml</code> locally. Review the generated file and configure its runner, credentials and approval settings before committing it to an application repository. Exporting does not enable a workflow, upload secrets or deploy the application. Other targets are <code>gitlab</code>, <code>azure</code> and <code>jenkins</code>. The <a href="reference.html#cli">CLI reference</a> also lists selecting an individual step and failure/success handlers.</p>
</section>

<section class="ref-sec" id="interop">
  <h2><a href="#interop">C and Python: reuse an existing library</a></h2>
  <p>Use interoperability when an existing library supplies behavior your application needs. Both integrations need the relevant host runtime or toolchain; an import is executable integration, not a declaration that automatically contains third-party code.</p>
  <h3 id="interop-python">Import a Python module</h3>
  <p><code>use python statistics as stats</code> loads Python’s standard-library module under a local name. Numbers, text, lists and maps can cross the boundary. This example needs no third-party Python package:</p>
{{run:interop/python.nexo}}
  <p>For another module, install it in the Python environment used by the Nexo integration and test its availability on the deployment host. A dependency installed in an unrelated virtual environment may not be visible there. Conversion support does not imply that arbitrary Python objects behave like Nexo records or that Python calls are pure.</p>
  <h3 id="interop-c">Check a C signature</h3>
  <p><code>use c "libm"</code> uses the system math library. C integration requires the supported C header discovery/toolchain and the library on the host. Keep headers and the linked library consistent.</p>
{{run:interop/c.nexo}}
  <p>Signature checking catches this incorrect argument before a valid C invocation can occur. The command is deliberately expected to fail:</p>
{{run:interop/bad-argument.nexo}}
  <h3 id="interop-boundaries">Audit the native boundary</h3>
{{steps:audit interop/c.nexo}}
  <p><code>audit</code> inventories known C imports and calls; it is not a vulnerability scanner or a proof of memory safety. Valid signatures cannot prevent a C library from corrupting memory. Review ownership, library versions and deployment requirements independently. Shield rejects in-process C and Python integrations even when the root policy grants them, because their confinement is outside the supported boundary. See the <a href="reference.html#mod-interop">interop reference</a> and <a href="shield.html#boundaries">Shield limitations</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="ref-sec" id="reliability">
  <h2><a href="#reliability">Resilience and workflows: make failure behavior explicit</a></h2>
  <p>These are language constructs and ordinary functions rather than extra modules to import. Decide whether an operation can safely be repeated, which failures are recoverable, and how completed side effects will be compensated.</p>
  <h3 id="reliability-retry">Bound retries and provide an explicit fallback</h3>
  <p>This local simulation tries at most twice, then returns a cached value. It verifies both recovery on the second attempt and exhaustion. The simulated provider has no external side effects and no delay; this is not a general retry decorator.</p>
{{run:reliability/retry.nexo}}
  <p>Real retry policy should select transient failures, bound elapsed time and account for repeated effects. A cached result has different freshness semantics from a successful live request: make that tradeoff explicit to callers. For built-in HTTP timeouts and method-specific retries, use the <a href="#web-client">HTTP client guide</a>. Do not stack retries without calculating the total attempt budget.</p>
  <h3 id="reliability-circuit">Stop calling a failing dependency</h3>
  <p><code>protect(provider, fails: 2, wait: 1m)</code> opens a circuit after two consecutive failures. While open, it raises <code>CircuitOpen</code> without invoking the protected block. The example’s third request takes that path; the one-minute window does not make this program sleep for a minute.</p>
{{run:reliability/circuit.nexo}}
  <p>The circuit’s local state is not a durable or distributed failure counter. Choose an appropriate fallback or propagate the failure. See the <a href="reference.html#patterns">patterns reference</a> for state machines and the surrounding constructs.</p>
  <h3 id="reliability-actions">Undo an application action</h3>
  <p>An <code>action</code> declares its forward operation and an <code>undo</code>. Calls are remembered for reversal in the current action history. Here two list edits are undone in reverse order; this history is not the durable workflow store.</p>
{{run:reliability/actions.nexo}}
  <h3 id="reliability-workflows">Compensate completed workflow steps</h3>
  <p>A <code>workflow</code> saves step progress in <code>.nexo/workflows/</code>. A failure runs the completed steps’ declared compensations in reverse order. The example creates an account, confirms it and sends a welcome message; the simulated failed delivery removes only that failed signup.</p>
{{run:reliability/workflow.nexo}}
  <p>State is saved after completed steps; a process interrupted between an external effect and its progress save may repeat that effect on restart. Use idempotent operations or external deduplication where required. The local journal is not a transaction with a remote service, and compensation does not automatically reverse arbitrary effects. Successful runs remove their workflow journal; calling again with the same arguments starts a new run.</p>
  <p>The sample tests compensation in one process, not a crash-recovery guarantee. A <code>step wait condition, max: ...</code> requires a deadline. Keep journal storage durable and protected when using restart recovery. Persistent workflows are rejected under Shield because their state confinement is not implemented. Continue with the <a href="reference.html#workflows">workflow reference</a> and <a href="shield.html#boundaries">execution boundaries</a>.</p>
</section>

